WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.300
A neuron takes in signals on its dendrites and sends its output along its axon.

2
00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:11.480
These branches differ in structure and molecules.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.400
That difference is neuronal polarity.

4
00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:17.840
Dendrites carry receptors for neurotransmitters.

5
00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:21.460
The axon has its own channels and vesicle machinery.

6
00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:25.620
The cell must send proteins to the right place and keep them there.

7
00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:29.580
How does it choose an axon, then keep these domains different?

8
00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:32.720
Start with a young rat neuron growing in a dish.

9
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:37.580
A rat hippocampal neuron grows several short neurites, all alike.

10
00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:40.800
After a day or two, one grows much faster.

11
00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:42.860
It becomes the axon.

12
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.080
The others grow as dendrites.

13
00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:47.340
The choice behaves like a race.

14
00:00:48.333 --> 00:00:51.953
Positive feedback makes the leader grow faster the more it grows.

15
00:00:52.713 --> 00:00:55.713
A lipid called PIP3 builds up at its tip.

16
00:00:56.613 --> 00:01:00.613
Blocking PI3-kinase is reported to stop axon formation.

17
00:01:01.873 --> 00:01:03.413
Negative feedback holds the others back.

18
00:01:04.253 --> 00:01:05.613
How it does that is less settled.

19
00:01:06.617 --> 00:01:11.497
At the growing tip, actin is looser and microtubules are more stable than in its

20
00:01:11.497 --> 00:01:17.497
siblings. Locally loosen actin, or stabilize microtubules, and a chosen neurite can

21
00:01:17.497 --> 00:01:20.937
become the axon. The cytoskeleton can tip the choice.

22
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:24.280
Those microtubules also guide cargo.

23
00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:27.700
In the axon, nearly all point plus-end-out.

24
00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:30.860
In vertebrate dendrites, they point both ways.

25
00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:36.540
A kinesin that prefers stable, plus-end-out tracks can steer cargo into the axon.

26
00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:41.300
Dynein can carry cargo outward on minus-end-out tracks in dendrites.

27
00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:45.100
Different tracks help keep different proteins in each domain.

28
00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:50.350
Near the cell body, ankyrin G builds the axon initial segment.

29
00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:53.830
In many neurons, action potentials start here.

30
00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:56.130
It also helps hold polarity.

31
00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:00.410
Lower ankyrin G after the segment forms, and it falls apart.

32
00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:04.290
The dendrite protein MAP2 enters the axon.

33
00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:09.490
The near end gains dendritic features, while farther out it keeps axonal proteins.

34
00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:12.170
Identity needs active maintenance.

35
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:16.800
In tissue, environmental cues help orient this machinery.

36
00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:22.460
Many cortical neurons form their axon from the trailing process during migration.

37
00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:30.180
Cortical neurons lacking LKB1, or both SAD kinases it activates, fail to form axons.

38
00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:32.640
The dish reveals mechanisms.

39
00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:36.360
The developing brain supplies a different starting point.
